<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- THE ARMING OF scripts/fleet-liveness-probe.sh (mg-f867).

         WHY THIS IS A LAUNCHD JOB AND NOT A SCHEDULE. This is the whole
         finding, so it is written here rather than left to the script:

         A detector hosted INSIDE the population it watches cannot report that
         population failing.

         `deploy-verify` §0 exists for exactly the outage of 2026-08-14, its own
         text asks the right question, and its completed-turn-artifact test would
         have caught the fleet stop on the morning of 08-15. It never ran,
         because it is `deploy-verify-architect` — one of architect's own
         schedules — and architect was one of the seven agents that stopped. That
         is a circularity, not a coverage gap: the check EXISTS, is CORRECT, and
         is armed on a nightly cadence, and it is disarmed by precisely the event
         it is designed to detect.

         So the three obvious homes are all forbidden:

           `pogo schedule`      pogod's scheduler delivers a nudge or a mail to
                                an AGENT; it cannot run a command. Arming this
                                way needs pogod alive AND an agent turn to
                                execute the instruction, and "turns that never
                                run" is the entire subject.
           a crew schedule      reproduces the circularity verbatim. This is what
                                deploy-verify §0 was, and it is why five days of
                                fleet outage went unreported.
           inside pogod         internal/turnwatch already reads this artifact
                                from inside pogod and is the right thing at its
                                own latency. It does not close POGOD WEDGED
                                rather than exited: a resident reader wedges with
                                its host, and launchd restarts on exit only. Its
                                own package header says so.

         launchd is triggered by the OS clock. It is independent of pogod, of the
         fleet, of the refinery, of the deploy and of any agent turn. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.pogo.fleetliveness</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Update YOUR_USERNAME, or use scripts/install-fleet-liveness-probe.sh.

             The program is the TRACKED script in a checkout, never a copy taken
             at install time. A copy would freeze at the revision that was
             current when somebody last remembered to re-install.

             deploy-src rather than ~/dev/pogo, for the same reason as
             com.pogo.revisionprobe: the latter is a working tree a human edits
             and can be mid-rebase, on a feature branch, or dirty at any hour. -->
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/.pogo/deploy-src/scripts/fleet-liveness-probe.sh</string>
        <string>--turnlog-dir</string>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/.pogo/agents/turnlog</string>
        <!-- TWO HOURS. Longer than any legitimate fleet-wide quiet period
             observed on this box, and far shorter than the two real outages
             (2026-08-11, 22h; 2026-08-14, ~118h).

             The predicate is NEWEST-ACROSS-ALL, never per-agent, and that is
             what makes one number safe: an idle PM legitimately goes hours
             between turns, and this box carries an `a270.log` untouched since
             2026-08-11 by design, so a per-agent threshold would page on both.
             The most recent turn by ANY agent being two hours old means every
             agent is down simultaneously. -->
        <string>--stale-after</string>
        <string>2h</string>
        <!-- The stamp carries the re-notify record. Named explicitly rather
             than left to $HOME resolution: an unattended job that silently picks
             a different stamp path than the hand-run does has a throttle that
             resets every time an operator checks on it. -->
        <string>--stamp</string>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/.pogo/fleet-liveness.stamp</string>
        <!-- IT REPORTS EITHER WAY. One ledger line per run, green, red or
             unmeasurable, so that "no alert" and "no probe" stop being the same
             observation. The newest line's age is the only thing on this box
             that answers "is the witness itself still firing?" — and a witness
             that was not firing is what this ticket is about. -->
        <string>--log</string>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/Library/Logs/pogo/fleet-liveness.log</string>
        <!-- The quiet flag trims the NARRATIVE, never the ledger and never an
             alert. The ALERT headline is a bare echo and survives it;
             unmeasurable findings and setup failures go to stderr and survive it
             too. (XML comments may not contain a double hyphen, which is why
             flags are named in prose rather than quoted literally.) -->
        <string>--quiet</string>
        <!-- MAIL, NEVER NUDGE, and this was measured during the outage rather
             than assumed. Nudges recovered the three agents that were merely
             unreachable and did nothing for the three that were wedged: mayor
             never goes idle so the idle gate dropped its nudges, and pa answered
             a wake with silence so wake silence suppression stopped trying for
             106h. Mail is the only wake channel that survived both rules, and it
             is how architect itself recovered.

             It goes to `human` because during a fleet-wide stop there is by
             definition no in-fleet actor left to act — doctor, which would
             normally restart a wedged agent, was itself inert. -->
        <string>--mail</string>
    </array>

    <!-- EVERY FIFTEEN MINUTES, and the two numbers are chosen together.

         Unlike com.pogo.revisionprobe, the age here is NOT accumulated in a
         stamp: it is read straight off the file's mtime, so it is true on the
         first fire after a wake regardless of how many were missed. The sampling
         rate therefore buys DETECTION LATENCY and nothing else — worst case
         threshold + 15 minutes.

         The cost of sampling often is notification volume, and that is paid in
         the probe's renotify flag (default 2h, equal to the threshold, so a
         standing stop notifies at most once per threshold window) rather than
         here, because the sampling rate and the notification rate answer
         different questions and one schedule field cannot serve both.

         The minutes are off the hour boundary to stay clear of the deploy fires
         and the restart windows they open.

         REPLAY POLICY — DECLARED, because launchd has no field for it and an
         inherited policy is one nobody chose. StartCalendarInterval is
         DEFERRED-ONCE across sleep: any number of fires missed while asleep are
         delivered as one run on wake. That is the right policy here and the
         choice is not arbitrary — this probe's report is not a per-interval
         sample. It compares a file mtime against wall clock, so a report
         delivered late is still TRUE and still names the correct age. A skip
         policy would discard the first report after a wake, which is the report
         most likely to carry news; a count policy would replay ten identical
         reports of one fact.

         WHAT THIS POLICY CANNOT COVER, stated so nobody rediscovers it: a host
         that is POWERED OFF misses the fire outright, and launchd defers across
         sleep, not across shutdown. The ledger is how that silence is visible
         after the fact. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict><key>Minute</key><integer>7</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Minute</key><integer>22</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Minute</key><integer>37</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Minute</key><integer>52</integer></dict>
    </array>

    <!-- RunAtLoad=true. A fire of this job is a stat and a subtraction: it never
         builds, installs, restarts or writes anything but its own stamp and
         ledger. And the first thing anyone wants after arming a witness is
         evidence that it fires, which this provides at install time instead of
         up to fifteen minutes later. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- One-shot per fire. KeepAlive=true would relaunch it in a loop, since it
         exits on every run by design. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>

    <!-- Two files, deliberately. fleet-liveness.log is the LEDGER — one line per
         run, written from an EXIT trap, and it is the heartbeat. This one
         catches whatever the run said out loud, which under the quiet flag is
         alerts, unmeasurable findings and delivery-control failures only.
         Mixing them would leave the heartbeat's newest line buried under
         narrative. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/Library/Logs/pogo/fleet-liveness.report.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/Library/Logs/pogo/fleet-liveness.report.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- Must resolve `mg` and nothing else. It deliberately does NOT need
             `go`, `pogo` or `pogod`: this probe must run on a box where the
             fleet has been dead for five days, and reaching for anything an
             agent turn or a deploy provides would arm it only under the
             conditions it exists to report. ~/go/bin is on the path for `mg`
             alone, and scripts/fleet-liveness-probe.sh makes every candidate
             self-identify as macguffin before trusting it, because /usr/bin/mg
             is the Micro-Emacs editor. -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/go/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME</string>
        <!-- POGO_HOME is pinned rather than inherited. This box's shell exports
             the LEGACY value POGO_HOME=$HOME (an old integration meaning "where
             the dotfiles live"). The probe normalizes that the same way
             internal/config.PogoHome does, so either value works; pinning it
             removes the question from the job entirely. -->
        <key>POGO_HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/YOUR_USERNAME/.pogo</string>
        <!-- NO GH_TOKEN HERE, EVER. ~/Library/LaunchAgents is world-readable.
             This probe reads a local directory and writes local mail; it needs
             no credential of any kind. -->
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
